1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:03,820
Systems of equations show up
all over the SAT math section.

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:08,859
Two equations, two unknowns,
and a question about them.

3
00:00:08,879 --> 00:00:12,379
Most students can solve a basic system.

4
00:00:12,399 --> 00:00:14,729
The points get lost somewhere else.

5
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:19,109
They solve for x when the
question asks for x plus y.

6
00:00:19,129 --> 00:00:24,150
They spend two minutes on a
system that one move would finish.

7
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:28,910
And they can't tell no solution from
infinitely many without solving the whole

8
00:00:28,930 --> 00:00:29,930
thing.

9
00:00:30,107 --> 00:00:34,287
In this video, you'll learn both
methods and when to use each,

10
00:00:34,307 --> 00:00:39,726
the shortcut for x plus y questions,
how to count solutions just by looking,

11
00:00:39,746 --> 00:00:44,436
how to set up word problems,
and how Desmos checks all of it.

12
00:00:44,547 --> 00:00:47,997
Six practice questions,
getting harder as we go.

13
00:00:48,473 --> 00:00:51,533
First, what a solution actually is.

14
00:00:51,553 --> 00:00:55,053
A solution to a system
is one pair of values,

15
00:00:55,073 --> 00:01:00,443
an x and a y, that makes both
equations true at the same time.

16
00:01:00,886 --> 00:01:04,096
On a graph, each linear
equation is a line.

17
00:01:04,245 --> 00:01:07,056
The solution is where the two lines cross.

18
00:01:07,246 --> 00:01:09,626
That's why Desmos is so useful here.

19
00:01:09,646 --> 00:01:12,466
Type both equations,
click the intersection,

20
00:01:12,486 --> 00:01:13,656
and read the point.

21
00:01:14,082 --> 00:01:16,182
By hand, you have two tools.

22
00:01:16,202 --> 00:01:21,372
Substitution: solve one equation for
a variable and plug it into the other.

23
00:01:21,482 --> 00:01:26,772
Elimination: add or subtract the
equations so one variable cancels.

24
00:01:27,194 --> 00:01:28,484
Here's how to choose.

25
00:01:28,634 --> 00:01:31,374
If one equation already
says y equals something,

26
00:01:31,394 --> 00:01:33,934
or x equals something, substitute.

27
00:01:33,954 --> 00:01:37,614
If a pair of coefficients
already match or are opposites,

28
00:01:37,634 --> 00:01:38,493
eliminate.

29
00:01:38,513 --> 00:01:42,294
If neither is true, elimination
is usually faster anyway.

30
00:01:42,314 --> 00:01:44,084
You just multiply first.

31
00:01:44,586 --> 00:01:49,156
On the SAT, systems questions
come in four main types.

32
00:01:49,186 --> 00:01:52,915
Type one asks you to solve for x or y.

33
00:01:52,946 --> 00:01:56,876
Type two asks for a
combination, like x plus y.

34
00:01:57,288 --> 00:01:59,987
Type three asks how many
solutions there are,

35
00:02:00,007 --> 00:02:04,748
or for the value of a constant that
makes a system have no solution or

36
00:02:04,768 --> 00:02:06,218
infinitely many.

37
00:02:06,488 --> 00:02:10,948
Type four is a word problem, where
you build the system yourself.

38
00:02:10,968 --> 00:02:12,578
We'll do every type.

39
00:02:13,088 --> 00:02:14,338
Question one.

40
00:02:14,608 --> 00:02:18,148
In the system, y equals three x minus two,

41
00:02:18,168 --> 00:02:21,218
and two x plus y equals thirteen.

42
00:02:21,368 --> 00:02:24,268
If x, y is the solution to the system,

43
00:02:24,288 --> 00:02:26,497
what is the value of x?

44
00:02:26,944 --> 00:02:28,194
Pause and try it.

45
00:02:33,990 --> 00:02:36,970
The first equation already
says y equals something.

46
00:02:36,990 --> 00:02:39,040
That's the signal to substitute.

47
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:44,200
Replace y in the second
equation with three x minus two.

48
00:02:44,655 --> 00:02:47,155
Now it's one equation with one variable.

49
00:02:47,175 --> 00:02:53,194
Two x plus three x is five x, so
five x minus two equals thirteen.

50
00:02:53,214 --> 00:02:56,585
Add two, and five x equals fifteen.

51
00:02:56,654 --> 00:02:59,475
Divide by five, and x equals three.

52
00:02:59,495 --> 00:03:01,225
The answer is B.

53
00:03:01,679 --> 00:03:03,369
Look at the wrong choices.

54
00:03:03,519 --> 00:03:06,379
Fifteen is five x, one step too early.

55
00:03:06,399 --> 00:03:09,379
Seven is y, because three times three,

56
00:03:09,399 --> 00:03:11,298
minus two, is seven.

57
00:03:11,318 --> 00:03:15,859
And eleven fifths comes from
flipping the sign on the two.

58
00:03:15,879 --> 00:03:19,649
Each one is a real number
from a slightly wrong process.

59
00:03:20,078 --> 00:03:21,368
Question two.

60
00:03:21,558 --> 00:03:29,297
Three x plus two y equals seventeen,
and five x minus two y equals seven.

61
00:03:29,317 --> 00:03:31,217
The solution is x, y.

62
00:03:31,237 --> 00:03:33,407
What is the value of y?

63
00:03:33,928 --> 00:03:35,178
Pause and try it.

64
00:03:40,975 --> 00:03:43,155
Neither equation is solved for a variable.

65
00:03:43,175 --> 00:03:47,035
But look at the y terms:
plus two y and minus two y.

66
00:03:47,055 --> 00:03:48,275
They're opposites.

67
00:03:48,295 --> 00:03:51,304
Add the equations, and y disappears.

68
00:03:51,717 --> 00:03:54,407
Three x plus five x is eight x.

69
00:03:54,476 --> 00:03:57,207
Seventeen plus seven is twenty-four.

70
00:03:57,397 --> 00:04:01,327
So eight x equals twenty-four,
and x equals three.

71
00:04:01,767 --> 00:04:03,976
But the question asks for y.

72
00:04:04,327 --> 00:04:06,897
Plug three back into the first equation.

73
00:04:07,167 --> 00:04:12,027
Nine plus two y equals
seventeen, so two y equals eight,

74
00:04:12,047 --> 00:04:13,897
and y equals four.

75
00:04:14,326 --> 00:04:18,137
If you typed three, you answered
a question they didn't ask.

76
00:04:18,637 --> 00:04:19,377
Question three.

77
00:04:19,397 --> 00:04:26,007
Five x plus two y equals thirty-one, and
two x plus five y equals twenty-five.

78
00:04:26,117 --> 00:04:29,366
What is the value of x plus y?

79
00:04:29,806 --> 00:04:31,056
Pause and try it.

80
00:04:36,852 --> 00:04:39,191
When a question asks for a combination,

81
00:04:39,211 --> 00:04:42,512
don't rush to find x and y separately.

82
00:04:42,532 --> 00:04:44,032
Look at the coefficients first.

83
00:04:44,052 --> 00:04:47,991
They're flipped: five and
two, then two and five.

84
00:04:48,011 --> 00:04:50,741
When that happens, add the equations.

85
00:04:51,210 --> 00:04:53,779
Five x plus two x is seven x.

86
00:04:53,929 --> 00:04:56,630
Two y plus five y is seven y.

87
00:04:56,650 --> 00:04:59,269
And thirty-one plus
twenty-five is fifty-six.

88
00:04:59,289 --> 00:05:03,859
So seven x plus seven y equals fifty-six.

89
00:05:04,170 --> 00:05:08,820
Divide everything by seven,
and x plus y equals eight.

90
00:05:09,450 --> 00:05:11,220
The answer is C.

91
00:05:11,685 --> 00:05:15,334
Fifty-six is the trap
if you forget to divide.

92
00:05:15,404 --> 00:05:18,745
Two is what you get if you
subtract the equations.

93
00:05:18,765 --> 00:05:20,934
That gives x minus y.

94
00:05:21,125 --> 00:05:23,385
Useful, but not what they asked.

95
00:05:23,405 --> 00:05:26,014
And five is x by itself.

96
00:05:26,535 --> 00:05:27,595
Question four.

97
00:05:27,615 --> 00:05:31,195
A school club sold one hundred
twenty tickets to a play.

98
00:05:31,215 --> 00:05:33,915
Student tickets cost six dollars each,

99
00:05:33,935 --> 00:05:36,514
and adult tickets cost ten dollars each.

100
00:05:36,534 --> 00:05:40,035
The club collected a total of
nine hundred forty dollars.

101
00:05:40,055 --> 00:05:42,705
How many adult tickets did the club sell?

102
00:05:43,170 --> 00:05:44,420
Pause and try it.

103
00:05:51,216 --> 00:05:53,466
Name the unknowns first.

104
00:05:53,736 --> 00:05:56,426
Let s be the number of student tickets,

105
00:05:56,456 --> 00:05:59,945
and a be the number of adult tickets.

106
00:06:00,656 --> 00:06:04,076
Word problems like this
usually give you two totals:

107
00:06:04,096 --> 00:06:07,546
a total count and a total amount of money.

108
00:06:07,656 --> 00:06:10,346
Each total becomes one equation.

109
00:06:10,791 --> 00:06:14,601
The count equation is s plus
a equals one hundred twenty.

110
00:06:14,670 --> 00:06:19,921
The money equation is six s plus
ten a equals nine hundred forty.

111
00:06:20,790 --> 00:06:24,200
Each kind of ticket gets
multiplied by its price.

112
00:06:24,727 --> 00:06:26,387
Solve the first one for s.

113
00:06:26,407 --> 00:06:29,337
s equals one hundred twenty minus a.

114
00:06:29,567 --> 00:06:34,547
Substitute: six times the quantity
one hundred twenty minus a,

115
00:06:34,567 --> 00:06:37,827
plus ten a, equals nine hundred forty.

116
00:06:37,847 --> 00:06:46,457
That's seven hundred twenty plus
four a equals nine hundred forty.

117
00:06:46,517 --> 00:06:51,937
So four a equals two hundred
twenty, and a equals fifty-five.

118
00:06:51,957 --> 00:06:54,207
The answer is A.

119
00:06:54,649 --> 00:06:57,428
Sixty-five is the number
of student tickets,

120
00:06:57,448 --> 00:07:00,699
the trap for solving
for the wrong variable.

121
00:07:01,009 --> 00:07:06,138
In Desmos, let x be student
tickets and y be adult tickets.

122
00:07:06,249 --> 00:07:09,948
Type both equations, zoom out,
and click the intersection:

123
00:07:09,968 --> 00:07:12,059
sixty-five, fifty-five.

124
00:07:12,649 --> 00:07:14,269
Now type three.

125
00:07:14,289 --> 00:07:17,099
How many solutions does a system have?

126
00:07:17,369 --> 00:07:19,669
Two lines can only do three things.

127
00:07:19,689 --> 00:07:22,298
They cross once: one solution.

128
00:07:22,329 --> 00:07:24,589
They're parallel: no solution.

129
00:07:24,609 --> 00:07:27,138
Or they're the same line: infinitely many.

130
00:07:27,550 --> 00:07:30,050
You can tell which one without graphing.

131
00:07:30,070 --> 00:07:32,570
Write both equations in the same form,

132
00:07:32,590 --> 00:07:35,250
then compare the coefficients as ratios:

133
00:07:35,270 --> 00:07:38,959
x to x, y to y, and constant to constant.

134
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:43,560
If the x and y ratios are
different, there's one solution.

135
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:47,829
If the x and y ratios match
but the constants don't,

136
00:07:47,979 --> 00:07:51,350
the lines are parallel,
so there's no solution.

137
00:07:51,620 --> 00:07:55,080
If all three ratios
match, it's the same line,

138
00:07:55,100 --> 00:07:57,310
so there are infinitely many.

139
00:07:57,757 --> 00:07:59,057
Question five.

140
00:07:59,077 --> 00:08:02,167
How many solutions does this system have?

141
00:08:02,237 --> 00:08:09,207
Six x minus four y equals ten, and
nine x minus six y equals twelve.

142
00:08:09,714 --> 00:08:10,964
Pause and try it.

143
00:08:16,760 --> 00:08:18,380
Compare the ratios.

144
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:21,130
Six over nine is two thirds.

145
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:24,540
Negative four over negative
six is also two thirds.

146
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:26,979
So the lines have the same slope.

147
00:08:26,999 --> 00:08:28,450
Now the constants.

148
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:32,139
Ten over twelve is five
sixths, and that doesn't match.

149
00:08:32,159 --> 00:08:33,820
Same slope, different lines.

150
00:08:33,840 --> 00:08:35,860
They're parallel, so there's no solution.

151
00:08:35,880 --> 00:08:37,570
The answer is A.

152
00:08:38,030 --> 00:08:39,690
The trap is infinitely many.

153
00:08:39,710 --> 00:08:44,250
If you only check the x and y terms,
the equations look like multiples.

154
00:08:44,270 --> 00:08:46,129
Always check the constants too.

155
00:08:46,149 --> 00:08:48,770
And choice C is out from the start.

156
00:08:48,790 --> 00:08:52,320
Two different lines can
never cross exactly twice.

157
00:08:52,830 --> 00:08:54,650
Question six, the hardest one.

158
00:08:54,670 --> 00:08:59,730
In the system, one half x
plus two thirds y equals four,

159
00:08:59,750 --> 00:09:03,049
and k x plus eight y equals eleven.

160
00:09:03,069 --> 00:09:04,610
k is a constant.

161
00:09:04,630 --> 00:09:08,360
For what value of k does
the system have no solution?

162
00:09:08,779 --> 00:09:09,959
Pause and try it.

163
00:09:09,979 --> 00:09:11,589
Take a little longer on this one.

164
00:09:19,441 --> 00:09:21,941
No solution means parallel lines.

165
00:09:21,961 --> 00:09:26,061
So the x and y coefficients
have to be in the same ratio,

166
00:09:26,081 --> 00:09:28,341
but the constants can't be.

167
00:09:28,361 --> 00:09:31,500
The fractions make that
hard to see, so clear them.

168
00:09:31,520 --> 00:09:34,170
Multiply the whole first
equation by twelve.

169
00:09:34,653 --> 00:09:36,862
Twelve times one half is six.

170
00:09:37,093 --> 00:09:39,623
Twelve times two thirds is eight.

171
00:09:39,693 --> 00:09:42,143
Twelve times four is forty-eight.

172
00:09:42,253 --> 00:09:47,062
So the first equation becomes six
x plus eight y equals forty-eight.

173
00:09:48,339 --> 00:09:50,759
Now both equations have eight y.

174
00:09:50,779 --> 00:09:54,599
For the lines to be parallel, the
x coefficients have to match too,

175
00:09:54,619 --> 00:09:56,199
so k equals six.

176
00:09:56,219 --> 00:09:57,519
Check the constants.

177
00:09:57,539 --> 00:09:59,519
Forty-eight and eleven are different,

178
00:09:59,539 --> 00:10:01,159
so the lines never touch.

179
00:10:01,179 --> 00:10:03,029
The answer is six.

180
00:10:03,492 --> 00:10:04,742
Desmos shortcut.

181
00:10:05,092 --> 00:10:06,782
Type both equations.

182
00:10:07,052 --> 00:10:10,672
For the fractions, press the right
arrow after each denominator,

183
00:10:10,692 --> 00:10:13,422
so x and y don't end up on the bottom.

184
00:10:13,651 --> 00:10:16,262
Desmos offers a slider for k.

185
00:10:16,332 --> 00:10:18,791
Drag it until the lines are parallel.

186
00:10:18,811 --> 00:10:21,302
That happens at k equals six.

187
00:10:21,730 --> 00:10:22,730
One more note.

188
00:10:22,930 --> 00:10:26,190
If the question had asked for
infinitely many solutions,

189
00:10:26,210 --> 00:10:28,780
the constants would have to match too.

190
00:10:28,850 --> 00:10:34,660
The second equation would need to be
six x plus eight y equals forty-eight.

191
00:10:34,770 --> 00:10:37,020
Same idea, one extra check.

192
00:10:37,530 --> 00:10:39,420
Here's the test-day method.

193
00:10:39,650 --> 00:10:43,540
Step one: circle what
the question asks for.

194
00:10:43,570 --> 00:10:46,859
x, y, x plus y, or a constant.

195
00:10:46,890 --> 00:10:49,660
That sets your goal
before you write anything.

196
00:10:50,176 --> 00:10:52,995
Step two: scan the coefficients.

197
00:10:53,015 --> 00:10:56,276
A variable already alone means substitute.

198
00:10:56,296 --> 00:10:59,836
Matching or opposite
coefficients mean eliminate.

199
00:10:59,856 --> 00:11:03,196
Flipped coefficients
mean add, then divide.

200
00:11:03,216 --> 00:11:07,546
And a question about the number
of solutions means compare ratios.

201
00:11:07,981 --> 00:11:11,001
Step three: if it's messy,
or you want a check,

202
00:11:11,021 --> 00:11:14,831
type both equations into Desmos
and click the intersection.

203
00:11:15,141 --> 00:11:20,390
For word problems, write down what x and
y stand for before you type anything.

204
00:11:20,819 --> 00:11:21,909
Quick recap.

205
00:11:22,259 --> 00:11:24,749
A solution is where the lines cross.

206
00:11:24,859 --> 00:11:28,109
Substitute when a
variable is already alone.

207
00:11:28,139 --> 00:11:30,669
Eliminate when the coefficients line up.

208
00:11:30,739 --> 00:11:34,798
For x plus y, look for a
combination before you solve.

209
00:11:34,818 --> 00:11:37,119
And to count solutions, compare ratios,

210
00:11:37,139 --> 00:11:38,669
including the constants.

211
00:11:39,136 --> 00:11:44,516
Every question here was written for this
video in the style of the real test.

212
00:11:44,536 --> 00:11:49,115
For official practice, the College Board's
free Question Bank lets you filter by

213
00:11:49,135 --> 00:11:53,426
skill, including systems
of two linear equations.

214
00:11:53,857 --> 00:11:55,827
If this helped, subscribe.

215
00:11:56,377 --> 00:12:00,157
The next video is quadratics: the vertex,

216
00:12:00,177 --> 00:12:02,586
the roots, and the discriminant.
